["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Long-end rates are high not because policy is weak, but because AI data centers are competing for funds Kansas City Fed President Schmid said at Jackson Hole that the 30-year Treasury yield above 5% is the result of AI and data centers competing for funds; his personal view is that monetary conditions remain loose, and inflation running at mid-threes must be addressed. Odd Lots Odd Lots Kansas City Fed President Jeffrey Schmid on the First Jackson Hole of the Warsh Era Monetary policy Inflation Long-end rates Federal Reserve AI infrastructure Payment innovation atomic settlement r-star reaction function forward guidance dual mandate dissent\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-oddlots-长端利率高企不是政策软弱-是实体需求在抢资金/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">28:56</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Long-end rates are high not because policy is weak, but because AI data centers are competing for funds</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Kansas City Fed President Schmid said at Jackson Hole that the 30-year Treasury yield above 5% is the result of AI and data centers competing for funds; his personal view is that monetary conditions remain loose, and inflation running at mid-threes must be addressed.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Monetary policy</span><span class=\"tag\">Inflation</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"No Models, No Early Enterprise Sales: Winning Giants with Interface and Speed Cursor&#x27;s success is not about building its own model, but treating the product itself as a moat: no plugins, no early enterprise sales, no self-trained models—catching up from behind through iteration speed, hiring philosophy, and founder density. The a16z Show a16z Inside Cursor: The Anatomy of a Generational Startup Cursor AI coding startup strategy enterprise sales talent acquisition product philosophy self-serve back channel talent via acquisition monorepo\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-a16z-不做模型不早卖企业-用界面和速度赢下巨头/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7b2f032532c3a865-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">39:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The a16z Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>No Models, No Early Enterprise Sales: Winning Giants with Interface and Speed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Cursor&#x27;s success is not about building its own model, but treating the product itself as a moat: no plugins, no early enterprise sales, no self-trained models—catching up from behind through iteration speed, hiring philosophy, and founder density.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cursor</span><span class=\"tag\">AI coding</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Google Paid $10 Million for a Bankrupt Airline&#x27;s Data: Data Is the Moat in the AI Era In the AI era, a company&#x27;s most valuable asset is its data—Google paid $10 million for a bankrupt airline&#x27;s data; AI agents with legitimate access are the new biggest threat, and data infrastructure must be rebuilt. No Priors No Priors Rethinking Legacy Data Infrastructure with Eon Co-Founders Ofir Ehrlich and Gonen Stein Data Assets AI Security Enterprise Data AI Agents Cloud Migration NHI / Non-Human Identity PLG / Product-Led Growth Data Foundation\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-nopriors-数据是-ai-时代唯一护城河-合法权限代理最危险/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/c958bc100db9c41b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"N\"><span class=\"dur\">34:50</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">No Priors</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Google Paid $10 Million for a Bankrupt Airline&#x27;s Data: Data Is the Moat in the AI Era</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In the AI era, a company&#x27;s most valuable asset is its data—Google paid $10 million for a bankrupt airline&#x27;s data; AI agents with legitimate access are the new biggest threat, and data infrastructure must be rebuilt.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Data Assets</span><span class=\"tag\">AI Security</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Index Funds Pay 4% More on Rebalancing: Passive Investing Is More Than Just Indexing David Booth breaks down the difference between index funds and passive investing: index rebalancing pushes stock prices up 4%, true passivity is scientific pricing plus fine execution; the 10% annual stock return over a century is driven by humanity&#x27;s desire to improve life. The Rational Reminder Podcast Rational Reminder 50 Years of Evidence-Based Investing (with David Booth) | Rational Reminder 424 Passive investing Index funds Trading costs Investment philosophy Dimensional Jensen&#x27;s alpha soft dollar beta passive vs indexed\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-rationalreminder-指数基金为收盘价多付4-被动投资不只是指数/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/8948b5b54aada07c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">The Rational Reminder Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Index Funds Pay 4% More on Rebalancing: Passive Investing Is More Than Just Indexing</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">David Booth breaks down the difference between index funds and passive investing: index rebalancing pushes stock prices up 4%, true passivity is scientific pricing plus fine execution; the 10% annual stock return over a century is driven by humanity&#x27;s desire to improve life.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Passive investing</span><span class=\"tag\">Index funds</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"What the Fed fears most is not tariffs but unanchored inflation expectations Chicago Fed President Goolsbee identifies the biggest risk as inflation: after five and a half years above target, the AI narrative may push rates up in the short term rather than down; his key advice for rate-sensitive industries is to watch the data, not the stock market. Masters of Scale Masters of Scale Chicago Fed President on inflation, recession, and Trump’s attacks Inflation expectations Federal Reserve Interest rates AI bubble Productivity Macro decisions Forward guidance Core inflation Unanchoring of inflation expectations Stagflation Lump of labor fallacy FOMC\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-masterscale-通胀预期失锚-美联储最怕的不是关税/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b728f390557a15d4-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"M\"><span class=\"dur\">35:42</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Masters of Scale</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>What the Fed fears most is not tariffs but unanchored inflation expectations</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Chicago Fed President Goolsbee identifies the biggest risk as inflation: after five and a half years above target, the AI narrative may push rates up in the short term rather than down; his key advice for rate-sensitive industries is to watch the data, not the stock market.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Inflation expectations</span><span class=\"tag\">Federal Reserve</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Sunscreen Toxicity Claims Debunked: Entering Blood Isn&#x27;t Harmful, but Vitamin D Risk Is Real The most-cited evidence for &quot;sunscreen is toxic and causes cancer&quot; mostly involves absurd dosages or reversed causality; chemical sunscreen ingredients entering the blood doesn&#x27;t equal toxicity. But daily application does increase the risk of vitamin D deficiency in winter, and evidence for preventing melanoma in darker skin is especially weak. Science Vs Science Vs Sunscreen: Could It Be Toxic?? sunscreen vitamin D melanoma chemical filters evidence-based medicine skin cancer oxybenzone UV index Fitzpatrick scale SPF mineral sunscreen RCT\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-sciencevs-防晒霜不是毒药-但深肤色人群可能真不用防黑素瘤/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a3282246c3202bd6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">50:52</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Science Vs</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Sunscreen Toxicity Claims Debunked: Entering Blood Isn&#x27;t Harmful, but Vitamin D Risk Is Real</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The most-cited evidence for &quot;sunscreen is toxic and causes cancer&quot; mostly involves absurd dosages or reversed causality; chemical sunscreen ingredients entering the blood doesn&#x27;t equal toxicity. But daily application does increase the risk of vitamin D deficiency in winter, and evidence for preventing melanoma in darker skin is especially weak.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">sunscreen</span><span class=\"tag\">vitamin D</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"No Plate Tectonics, No Life: Earth Is a Lucky Planet Plate tectonics reshapes the Earth&#x27;s surface at the speed of fingernail growth, generating and destroying about 3 square kilometers of oceanic crust each year; it alters climate, isolates species, and even determines whether life exists—without it, there would be no life. In Our Time In Our Time Archive: Plate Tectonics Plate Tectonics Earth Science Origin of Life Scientific Revolution Gaia Theory Planetary Habitability Benioff zone seafloor spreading Vine–Matthews hypothesis mantle plume black smoker Gaia theory\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-inourtime-没有板块构造就没有生命-地球是幸运的行星/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/c3f143ddc011b622-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">44:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">In Our Time</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>No Plate Tectonics, No Life: Earth Is a Lucky Planet</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Plate tectonics reshapes the Earth&#x27;s surface at the speed of fingernail growth, generating and destroying about 3 square kilometers of oceanic crust each year; it alters climate, isolates species, and even determines whether life exists—without it, there would be no life.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Plate Tectonics</span><span class=\"tag\">Earth Science</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Ketogenic Diet for Mental Illness: It&#x27;s Not About Weight Loss, It&#x27;s Mitochondria Psychiatrist Chris Palmer uses ketogenic and low-carb diets to help patients with treatment-resistant mental illness; the key isn&#x27;t just weight loss but changing brain energy metabolism and neurotransmitters through mitophagy and biogenesis. Huberman Lab Huberman Lab Essentials: Diet &amp; Nutrition for Mental Health | Dr. Chris Palmer ketogenic diet mental health mitochondria low-carb clinical cases Mitophagy Mitochondrial biogenesis Ketosis Schizoaffective disorder\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-huberman-生酮饮食治疗难治性精神疾病-线粒体是核心/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/10ab5bacdd6a5f8c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">38:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Huberman Lab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Ketogenic Diet for Mental Illness: It&#x27;s Not About Weight Loss, It&#x27;s Mitochondria</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Psychiatrist Chris Palmer uses ketogenic and low-carb diets to help patients with treatment-resistant mental illness; the key isn&#x27;t just weight loss but changing brain energy metabolism and neurotransmitters through mitophagy and biogenesis.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">ketogenic diet</span><span class=\"tag\">mental health</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The 15,000 in the Cobalt Mine: Your Phone Battery Is Stained with Blood The lithium-ion batteries in smartphones and EVs depend on Congolese cobalt; from Leopold II&#x27;s rubber terror to Lumumba&#x27;s assassination to today&#x27;s few dollars a day, the same history of plunder continues. Throughline Throughline The pillage that fueled America&#x27;s tech booms cobalt Congo supply chain colonial history EVs Congo Free State Katanga Congo Reform Association\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-throughline-没有刚果的钴-就没有你的智能手机/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/19b38564c3e319e4-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">52:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Throughline</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The 15,000 in the Cobalt Mine: Your Phone Battery Is Stained with Blood</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The lithium-ion batteries in smartphones and EVs depend on Congolese cobalt; from Leopold II&#x27;s rubber terror to Lumumba&#x27;s assassination to today&#x27;s few dollars a day, the same history of plunder continues.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">cobalt</span><span class=\"tag\">Congo</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"AI Detection Is Really Authorship Attribution, and Template-Letter Games Are Dead AI detection is really large-scale authorship attribution, not catching an &quot;AI smell.&quot; What&#x27;s truly disrupted are social processes that assume &quot;writing a letter&quot; costs human effort—credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant reviews. Once friction vanishes, institutions overload. Complex Systems Complex Systems How AI detection actually works, with Pangram’s Max Spero AI detection text provenance anti-spam public services coding careers model ethics anti-memetic property mode collapse cognitive surplus dead internet theory Jevons Paradox humanizer\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-complexsys-ai-检测的本质是作者识别-模板信博弈已失效/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d5836ffb777d7c36-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">1:04:54</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Complex Systems</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI Detection Is Really Authorship Attribution, and Template-Letter Games Are Dead</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI detection is really large-scale authorship attribution, not catching an &quot;AI smell.&quot; What&#x27;s truly disrupted are social processes that assume &quot;writing a letter&quot; costs human effort—credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant reviews. Once friction vanishes, institutions overload.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI detection</span><span class=\"tag\">text provenance</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Open-source models pass through five or six intermediaries, who fills the accountability gap in the EU AI Act? Open weights do not equal open trust; after passing through five or six intermediaries, responsibility for open-source models becomes blurred, and the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemptions may leave a structural accountability gap. Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital. The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast Building Trustworthy Open Source AI - Sebnem Erener (EP 38) Open Source EU AI Act AI Governance Open Source Ethics European Tech Sovereignty Chain of Responsibility zero trust open washing general purpose AI models layered responsibility\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-theinbetweente-开放权重不等于可信-五六个中介后谁来负责/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/99f7b27239fc36aa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">23:46</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Open-source models pass through five or six intermediaries, who fills the accountability gap in the EU AI Act?</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Open weights do not equal open trust; after passing through five or six intermediaries, responsibility for open-source models becomes blurred, and the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemptions may leave a structural accountability gap. Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Open Source</span><span class=\"tag\">EU AI Act</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics History Is Not a Prelude to the Nuclear Test: Postwar State Intervention Is the Real Watershed India&#x27;s early nuclear physics was in sync with the West; the real watershed came with postwar state intervention. Hindsight from the 1974 nuclear test only obscures the damage the TIFR model did to universities and the scientific system. The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science Jahnavi Phalkey on India&#x27;s Place in the History of Modern Science history of science India big science nuclear physics cyclotron state and science Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (TIFR) Department of Atomic Energy (DAE) Coulomb excitation Big Science global majority CERN\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-thehpspodcastc-别用1974年核试验倒读印度核物理史/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d0bc1b9f1ca1b0b2-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">46:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics History Is Not a Prelude to the Nuclear Test: Postwar State Intervention Is the Real Watershed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">India&#x27;s early nuclear physics was in sync with the West; the real watershed came with postwar state intervention. Hindsight from the 1974 nuclear test only obscures the damage the TIFR model did to universities and the scientific system.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">history of science</span><span class=\"tag\">India</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Agents Accidentally Breach Their Own Systems: Fully Automated Attacks Are Now a Reality During an internal security evaluation at OpenAI, multiple AI agents collaborated via a shared message board, accidentally breached their own Artifactory, and the incident spilled over to HuggingFace; the event proves that fully automated attacks are now a reality, and defenses must be equally automated. The AI Policy Podcast The AI Policy Podcast Responding to AI Agent Containment Failures with CSET&#x27;s Helen Toner, LawAI&#x27;s Mackenzie Arnold &amp; CSIS&#x27;s Matt Pearl AI Security Agent Collaboration Zero-Day Vulnerabilities Incident Response Cloud Infrastructure HuggingFace Artifactory SSRF RCE TOCTOU WebDAV IMDS\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-theaipolicypod-ai-agent-协作失控-安全评估意外攻破内外网/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/bc33d180be3b8ef6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:58</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The AI Policy Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI Agents Accidentally Breach Their Own Systems: Fully Automated Attacks Are Now a Reality</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">During an internal security evaluation at OpenAI, multiple AI agents collaborated via a shared message board, accidentally breached their own Artifactory, and the incident spilled over to HuggingFace; the event proves that fully automated attacks are now a reality, and defenses must be equally automated.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI Security</span><span class=\"tag\">Agent Collaboration</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The Vandals Were Not Barbarians: Cutting Off Tax Revenue Was What Killed the Western Roman Empire Early on, the Vandals were a small tribe that lost nine out of ten battles in Roman records, yet by seizing North Africa and cutting off Rome&#x27;s tax revenue, they sacked Rome in 455; the word &quot;vandalism&quot; was a political metaphor invented during the French Revolution. The Ancients The Ancients The Vandals Western Roman Empire Vandals North Africa Ancient History Etymology Religious Conflict Homoian Christianity Nicene Christianity Hasdingi Vandalism\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-ancients-十战九败的汪达尔人-靠断供税收终结西罗马/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/2e9a1e98d4f9d33b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">50:01</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Ancients</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The Vandals Were Not Barbarians: Cutting Off Tax Revenue Was What Killed the Western Roman Empire</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Early on, the Vandals were a small tribe that lost nine out of ten battles in Roman records, yet by seizing North Africa and cutting off Rome&#x27;s tax revenue, they sacked Rome in 455; the word &quot;vandalism&quot; was a political metaphor invented during the French Revolution.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Western Roman Empire</span><span class=\"tag\">Vandals</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"WWI Air Combat Was Not Romance, It Was a Meat Grinder Where Pilots Lasted Two Weeks on Average At the start of WWI, aircraft were dismissed as a joke by the officer class; within four years, the sky became a meat grinder of reconnaissance, bombing, and dogfighting, with new pilots in 1917 averaging only about two weeks of life. Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit History Hit The Brutal History of The First Warplanes WWI air combat technology iteration fighter aces military history psychological trauma Pusher Tractor Interrupter gear Scout Flying circus\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-historyhit-一战空战浪漫背后-新飞行员平均活两周/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4fac589db0b89acd-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">50:55</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>WWI Air Combat Was Not Romance, It Was a Meat Grinder Where Pilots Lasted Two Weeks on Average</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">At the start of WWI, aircraft were dismissed as a joke by the officer class; within four years, the sky became a meat grinder of reconnaissance, bombing, and dogfighting, with new pilots in 1917 averaging only about two weeks of life.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">WWI air combat</span><span class=\"tag\">technology iteration</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Britain Would Have Accepted Something Like the Belfast Agreement at Almost Any Point By 1972 the Heath cabinet already had the options on the table — ending partition, some constitutional link with Ireland. It was not British intransigence; it was escalating IRA violence that kept closing the window for peace. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 700. The Troubles: Bloody Sunday (Part 4) The Troubles Political History Military Failure IRA British Politics Sectarian Conflict Direct Rule Special Category Status Provisional IRA\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-restishistory-英国当年几乎随时接受类似-贝尔法斯特协议-的安排/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4d082c6220b28920-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:24:07</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Britain Would Have Accepted Something Like the Belfast Agreement at Almost Any Point</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">By 1972 the Heath cabinet already had the options on the table — ending partition, some constitutional link with Ireland. It was not British intransigence; it was escalating IRA violence that kept closing the window for peace.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">The Troubles</span><span class=\"tag\">Political History</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Proofs Close In on the Square-Root Barrier, Worst-Case Lattices Still Safe An OpenAI model proved CVP stays NP-hard under an approximation factor of n^(1/400), and within days the result was pushed to the square root; the exact-SVP exponent improved to 2^0.7n and McEliece took a quasi-polynomial attack, yet real-world lattice crypto is safe for now thanks to dimension blowup and the gap in approximation factors. Security Cryptography Whatever Security Cryptography Whatever AI Lattice Proofs with Chris Peikert lattice crypto post-quantum crypto AI mathematics cryptanalysis quantum algorithms McEliece CVP / Closest Vector Problem SVP / Shortest Vector Problem Dihedral coset problem Reed-Solomon code Information set decoding Doubly efficient PIR\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-securitycrypto-ai-证明封死-cvp-近似困难-gap-但不烧密码学/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a0ce9b13c93eb0a9-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">50:44</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Security Cryptography Whatever</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI Proofs Close In on the Square-Root Barrier, Worst-Case Lattices Still Safe</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">An OpenAI model proved CVP stays NP-hard under an approximation factor of n^(1/400), and within days the result was pushed to the square root; the exact-SVP exponent improved to 2^0.7n and McEliece took a quasi-polynomial attack, yet real-world lattice crypto is safe for now thanks to dimension blowup and the gap in approximation factors.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">lattice crypto</span><span class=\"tag\">post-quantum crypto</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"A programmer&#x27;s value shifts to orchestrating agents: DHH wrote an entire product with AI DHH has already written all of Omachi&#x27;s code with AI, and found a counterintuitive result: agents don&#x27;t need verbose instructions, they need to run in parallel. What is genuinely scarce is judgment, taste and architectural constraint. Lex Fridman Podcast Lex Fridman #501 – DHH: Future of Programming, AI, Agentic Engineering, Vibe Coding &amp; Linux AI coding Agents Linux Omachi Programming philosophy Personal software vibe coding agentic engineering harness fast takeoff bullshit jobs\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-lex-程序员的价值从写码转向调度-agent/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d67cbc9ec26fc241-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">5:21:57</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Lex Fridman Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>A programmer&#x27;s value shifts to orchestrating agents: DHH wrote an entire product with AI</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">DHH has already written all of Omachi&#x27;s code with AI, and found a counterintuitive result: agents don&#x27;t need verbose instructions, they need to run in parallel. What is genuinely scarce is judgment, taste and architectural constraint.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI coding</span><span class=\"tag\">Agents</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Software Is 10x Slower Because of Architecture, Not Code Software runs 10 to 100 times slower than it needs to, and the root cause is not coding technique but serial dependencies at the architecture layer; correct optimization means fixing the hardware&#x27;s theoretical peak and then measuring the gap — which requires that whoever makes the architecture decisions understands assembly. The Pragmatic Engineer The Pragmatic Engineer Why performant code matters (but gets widely ignored), with Casey Muratori Performance Architecture Decisions Theoretical Peak AI Coding Compiler Optimization theoretical peak serial dependency chain hotspot branch prediction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-pragmatic-性能优化不靠剖析热点-靠理论峰值反推差距/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b99e79b803441ca6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:52:59</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Pragmatic Engineer</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Software Is 10x Slower Because of Architecture, Not Code</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Software runs 10 to 100 times slower than it needs to, and the root cause is not coding technique but serial dependencies at the architecture layer; correct optimization means fixing the hardware&#x27;s theoretical peak and then measuring the gap — which requires that whoever makes the architecture decisions understands assembly.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Performance</span><span class=\"tag\">Architecture Decisions</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"edu\" data-hay=\"AI&#x27;s real shortage isn&#x27;t compute—it&#x27;s self-doubt Large models aren&#x27;t without probability—they predict probabilities at every step—but they don&#x27;t treat uncertainty as a tool for seeing their own limits. To make real decisions, you first have to build self-doubt into the architecture. Google DeepMind Google DeepMind The mathematics of AI uncertainty Uncertainty Bayesian Self-doubt Large models Weather forecasting Trustworthy AI Bayesian updating aleatoric uncertainty semantic entropy calibration catastrophic forgetting\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-googledeepmind-ai-真正缺的不是算力-是自我怀疑/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b3109e2d16c60ca5-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"G\"><span class=\"dur\">45:04</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"edu\"><span class=\"src\">Google DeepMind</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI&#x27;s real shortage isn&#x27;t compute—it&#x27;s self-doubt</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Large models aren&#x27;t without probability—they predict probabilities at every step—but they don&#x27;t treat uncertainty as a tool for seeing their own limits. To make real decisions, you first have to build self-doubt into the architecture.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Uncertainty</span><span class=\"tag\">Bayesian</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI&#x27;s next step isn&#x27;t bigger models, it&#x27;s learning to admit uncertainty DeepMind researcher Zubin draws on 30 years of research to show that today&#x27;s large models make things up confidently because they lack any explicit representation of probability; Bayesian updating, forecast ensembles and calibration are what reliable AI and AGI actually turn on. Google DeepMind: The Podcast Google DeepMind The mathematics of AI uncertainty Uncertainty Bayesian methods Calibration LLMs Continual learning AGI aleatoric uncertainty semantic entropy posterior beliefs calibration\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-deepmind-ai-的下一步不是更大模型-而是学会承认不确定/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d9e4ddf810493203-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"G\"><span class=\"dur\">44:40</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Google DeepMind: The Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI&#x27;s next step isn&#x27;t bigger models, it&#x27;s learning to admit uncertainty</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">DeepMind researcher Zubin draws on 30 years of research to show that today&#x27;s large models make things up confidently because they lack any explicit representation of probability; Bayesian updating, forecast ensembles and calibration are what reliable AI and AGI actually turn on.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Uncertainty</span><span class=\"tag\">Bayesian methods</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"There Is No Foundation Model for Physics — Neural Operators Start Filling That Gap With Weather Anima Anandkumar brings weather forecasting into the AI era with neural operators: comparable accuracy at tens of thousands of times the speed, on a single consumer GPU — and shows how the physical world can reach foundation models with little data plus geometric inductive bias. Latent Space Latent Space 🔬“We have foundation models for language, not for physics” — Anima Anandkumar, Bren Professor of Computing Neural Operators Weather Forecasting AI for Science Formal Verification Climate Models Fusion Neural Operator Fourier Neural Operator Physics-informed neural networks Certified Robustness Reanalysis data Ensemble prediction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-latentspace-物理没有基础模型-神经算子正在补上这一课/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a6cc1a84e9a9a0c3-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">1:23:31</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Latent Space</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>There Is No Foundation Model for Physics — Neural Operators Start Filling That Gap With Weather</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Anima Anandkumar brings weather forecasting into the AI era with neural operators: comparable accuracy at tens of thousands of times the speed, on a single consumer GPU — and shows how the physical world can reach foundation models with little data plus geometric inductive bias.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Neural Operators</span><span class=\"tag\">Weather Forecasting</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Not Using AI Is Refusing the Internet in 1995: A Hedge Fund Makes Training Mandatory Walleye&#x27;s CEO made AI use mandatory for every employee, saying refusing AI is like refusing the internet in 1995. AI is leverage, not cheating, and most employees at future companies will be AI bots. Every Every Why This $10B Hedge Fund Made AI Training Mandatory (Best of the Pod) AI transformation Hedge funds Mandatory training Data strategy Quant trading Borg pass-through structure\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-every-对冲基金ceo-全员强制ai培训-不用ai就是1995年不上网/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d2f59d3bd0bebf0c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:07:33</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Every</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Not Using AI Is Refusing the Internet in 1995: A Hedge Fund Makes Training Mandatory</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Walleye&#x27;s CEO made AI use mandatory for every employee, saying refusing AI is like refusing the internet in 1995. AI is leverage, not cheating, and most employees at future companies will be AI bots.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI transformation</span><span class=\"tag\">Hedge funds</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Isn&#x27;t a Personal Tool — It&#x27;s an Organizational Change Leaders Must Model Themselves AI is not a personal tool; it is an organizational change that leaders have to demonstrate personally. At Walleye, everyone uses AI every day and every meeting gets recorded, building a first-mover advantage out of operating leverage and a data lake. AI &amp; I AI &amp; I（Every） A $10B Hedge Fund’s AI Playbook (Best of the Pod) Organizational change Hedge funds Firm-wide training Data lake Faster writing AI culture Borg operating leverage pass-through structure\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-aiandi-对冲基金-ceo-不用-ai-等于-1995-年不用互联网/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/979c8913b42df2b7-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">1:07:06</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">AI &amp; I</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI Isn&#x27;t a Personal Tool — It&#x27;s an Organizational Change Leaders Must Model Themselves</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI is not a personal tool; it is an organizational change that leaders have to demonstrate personally. At Walleye, everyone uses AI every day and every meeting gets recorded, building a first-mover advantage out of operating leverage and a data lake.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Organizational change</span><span class=\"tag\">Hedge funds</span>\n</div></div></a>"]